    {
      "label": "Instrumental calibration drift (mechanical/thermal change)",
      "probability": 0.35,
      "rationale": "A subtle change in pendulum length or suspension due to temperature or mechanical relaxation could shift the period, producing the observed cluster and occasional repeat values.",
      "evidenceFor": [130962, 197073, 781686, 221930, 36205, 476724, 520669],
      "evidenceAgainst": [770646, 959797] 
    },
    {
      "label": "Environmental periodic influence (e.g., daily temperature cycle)",
      "probability": 0.20,
      "rationale": "If ambient temperature cycles on a roughly 6‑day timescale, it could cause short‑term variations in pendulum length, explaining the drift without affecting the resonator as strongly.",
      "evidenceFor": [130962, 197073, 781686, 221930, 36205, 476724, 520669],
      "evidenceAgainst": [770646, 959797, 1045373, 336581] 
    },
    {
      "label": "Data‑logging artifact (duplicate output or rounding)",
      "probability": 0.30,
      "rationale": "The exact duplication of 1.6367 beats in two consecutive trials suggests a possible software or sensor buffering error that repeats the previous measurement.",
      "evidenceFor": [197073, 781686],
      "evidenceAgainst": [] 
    },
    {
      "label": "Random statistical fluctuation within baseline variance",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "Given the resolution (0.0001) and number of trials, occasional clustering or even an exact repeat can occur by chance, though the probability is low.",
      "evidenceFor": [],
      "evidenceAgainst": [197073, 781686] 
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.05,
  "summaryOfChange": "Previously no active hypotheses existed; the new question focuses on the unexpected repeat and clustering in pendulum_lab readings on day 10. Four plausible explanations have been formulated, probabilities allocated afresh (no prior weighting), and evidence from the day's event IDs has been attached to each hypothesis. The residual accounts for any other unknown factor."
}

